---
import { FirstSplitExplorer } from "@grove/tree-visuals";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How decision trees learn"
  description="A visual introduction to how classification trees choose questions and split data."
  wide
>
  <header class="page-header">
    <p class="eyebrow">Decision trees · Chapter 1</p>
    <h1>How does a decision tree learn?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A decision tree learns by asking a sequence of simple questions. Its job is
      to choose questions that separate the training examples into cleaner groups.
    </p>
  </header>

  <article class="lesson-body">
    <h2>Begin with one question</h2>
    <p>
      Imagine ten observations described by two numeric features. Each one belongs
      to <strong>Class A</strong> or <strong>Class B</strong>. At the root, all ten
      are mixed together.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tree tries questions such as <code>Feature 1 ≤ 2.75?</code>. Every
      possible threshold creates a left group and a right group. The tree scores
      those groups and keeps the question that reduces impurity the most.
    </p>
  </article>

  <div class="interactive-breakout">
    <FirstSplitExplorer client:load />
  </div>

  <article class="lesson-body">
    <h2>What just happened?</h2>
    <p>
      The dashed line and the root node come from the same trained model. Samples
      on the “yes” side move to the left leaf; samples on the “no” side move to
      the right leaf. Each leaf predicts its majority class.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Information gain</strong> is the parent impurity minus the weighted
      impurity of both children. Larger gain means the question produced cleaner
      groups. A full tree repeats this process inside every new node until a
      stopping rule says to create a leaf.
    </p>
    <p>
      This first version deliberately trains a one-level tree, also called a
      <strong>decision stump</strong>. It gives us a small, testable unit that can
      later be reused recursively to grow a complete tree.
    </p>
  </article>

  <a class="next-link" href={`${base}/learn/gini-vs-entropy`}>
    <span>Next chapter<strong>Gini or entropy?</strong></span>
    <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
  </a>
</BaseLayout>

